    "lawCode": "Amd §390-c, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5162\n\nSPONSOR: BRISPORT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to requiring local\nsocial service districts to offer to pay childcare providers via a\ndirect deposit for any subsidized child care funds owed\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 390-c of the social services law to\nauthorize and direct the Commissioner of the Office of Children and\nFamily Services to issue a regulation within one year of the effective\ndate of this section to require that local social service districts\ncreate an option for child care providers to be paid for any child care\nsubsidy funding owed via a direct deposit.\n\nSection 2 of the bill sets forth the effective date. Click here to enter\ntext.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation will give child care providers who receive subsidy\npayments from a local social services district the option to be paid by\na direct deposit. This will create a more streamlined system for both\nthe provider and the district.\n\nUnder the current system, a provider submits documentation to the county\nin order to get paid for care provided to children in their program that\nare eligible for a child care subsidy. They then wait, sometimes more\nthan four weeks, for their payment from the county. In addition, the\ndelivery of their mail is delayed during high volume mail periods, lost\nmail, or having mail stolen from a mailbox. They then have to wait even\nlonger for a new check to be issued, which is hard for a provider who is\nalready living paycheck to paycheck.  Many of the providers who receive\nsubsidy funds from local social services districts live in communities\nthat are traditionally underserved by banks. The lack of access to\nfinancial institutions creates a burden for these individuals to deposit\ntheir checks.  Instead, they may turn to other entities that are in\ntheir neighborhood, such as check cashing centers, that charges higher\nrates and fees.\n\nLastly, this legislation will create savings for local social service\ndistricts by eliminating the need to issue paper checks and the time\nassociated with such tasks.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
